      <colspec colname='c2'/>
<tbody>
<row><entry><indexterm><primary>aiPrimitiveType_POINT</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>aiPrimitiveType_POINT</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03fafb9158b4924ec9457647c759aa961053"/>aiPrimitiveType_POINT</entry><entry>
<para>A point primitive.</para>

<para>This is just a single vertex in the virtual world, <link linkend="_structai_face">aiFace</link> contains just one index for such a primitive. </para>
</entry></row><row><entry><indexterm><primary>aiPrimitiveType_LINE</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>aiPrimitiveType_LINE</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03fa724184f90f0e408b903d07aed39914b4"/>aiPrimitiveType_LINE</entry><entry>
<para>A line primitive.</para>

<para>This is a line defined through a start and an end position. <link linkend="_structai_face">aiFace</link> contains exactly two indices for such a primitive. </para>
</entry></row><row><entry><indexterm><primary>aiPrimitiveType_TRIANGLE</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>aiPrimitiveType_TRIANGLE</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03fae1875196046a6139abb8734fe97d67f5"/>aiPrimitiveType_TRIANGLE</entry><entry>
<para>A triangular primitive.</para>

<para>A triangle consists of three indices. </para>
</entry></row><row><entry><indexterm><primary>aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03faea762261e9efd0313baa0b3193b43253"/>aiPrimitiveType_POLYGON</entry><entry>
<para>A higher-level polygon with more than 3 edges.</para>

<para>A triangle is a polygon, but polygon in this context means &quot;all polygons that are not triangles&quot;. The &quot;Triangulate&quot;-Step is provided for your convenience, it splits all polygons in triangles (which are much easier to handle). </para>
</entry></row><row><entry><indexterm><primary>aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03fa9936ab10ebebd878a5cd6521b5bdc003"/>aiPrimitiveType_NGONEncodingFlag</entry><entry>
<para>A flag to determine whether this triangles only mesh is NGON encoded.</para>

<para>NGON encoding is a special encoding that tells whether 2 or more consecutive triangles should be considered as a triangle fan. This is identified by looking at the first vertex index. 2 consecutive triangles with the same 1st vertex index are part of the same NGON.</para>

<para>At the moment, only quads (concave or convex) are supported, meaning that polygons are &apos;seen&apos; as triangles, as usual after a triangulation pass.</para>

<para>To get an NGON encoded mesh, please use the aiProcess_Triangulate post process.</para>

<para><formalpara><title>See also</title>

<para><link linkend="_adhed_2assimp_2postprocess_8h_1a64795260b95f5a4b3f3dc1be4f52e410a9c3de834f0307f31fa2b1b6d05dd592b">aiProcess_Triangulate</link> <link linkend="_">https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF/pull/1620</link></para>
</formalpara>
</para>
</entry></row><row><entry><indexterm><primary>_aiPrimitiveType_Force32Bit</primary><secondary>mesh.h</secondary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>mesh.h</primary><secondary>_aiPrimitiveType_Force32Bit</secondary></indexterm>
<anchor xml:id="_adhed_2assimp_2mesh_8h_1ac352902280db7c3bd8ba64c133b7f03fa6cc0d20689875344a59dc56d8baf08f8"/>_aiPrimitiveType_Force32Bit</entry><entry>
<para>This value is not used. It is just here to force the compiler to map this enum to a 32 Bit integer. </para>
</entry></row>    </tbody>
    </tgroup>
</informaltable>
